            ## SCENARIO NARRATIVE:
            ## Phase 1: Initial Access — Device Code Phishing (T1566.002)
A threat actor sends a spearphishing email to a mid-level employee (victim_user@contoso.com)
containing a device code authorization URL. The email impersonates an IT notification
requesting the user to "verify their device" at microsoft.com/devicelogin. The victim enters
the attacker-supplied device code and completes authentication, unknowingly granting the
attacker a delegated OAuth access token and refresh token.

**Log evidence (AADSignInLogs):**
- AuthenticationProtocol = "deviceCode"
- ResultType = "0" (success)
- UserPrincipalName = "victim_user@contoso.com"
- IPAddress = attacker's external IP (203.0.113.42)
- AppDisplayName = "Microsoft Office"
- ResourceDisplayName = "Microsoft Graph"
- IsInteractive = true
- RiskLevelDuringSignIn may show "medium" or "high" due to anomalous IP

## Phase 2: Discovery — Directory and Application Enumeration (T1087.004)
Using the victim's delegated OAuth token, the attacker queries Microsoft Graph:
- GET /v1.0/servicePrincipals — lists all service principals with their appRoleAssignments
- GET /v1.0/users — enumerates directory users to identify CFO target

The attacker identifies "HR-Automation-App" with Mail.ReadWrite.All application permission
(appRole assignment to Microsoft Graph resource). This application was granted admin consent
but is rarely actively used, making credential injection less likely to be noticed.

**Log evidence (AADSignInLogs):**
- ResourceDisplayName = "Microsoft Graph"
- AppDisplayName = "Microsoft Office" (victim's token)
- Multiple rapid API calls from same CorrelationId
- UserAgent may show "python-requests" indicating scripted access

## Phase 3: Persistence — Credential Injection via addPassword (T1098.001)
The attacker exploits the victim's Application Administrator role to call:
POST /v1.0/servicePrincipals/{hr-app-sp-id}/addPassword

This injects a new client secret with a long expiry (2 years). The victim's elevated role
was assigned 6 months ago for a legitimate project but never revoked (PW-001).

**Log evidence (AADSignInLogs):**
- OperationName = "Add service principal credentials"
- Category = "ApplicationManagement"
- ActivityDisplayName = "Add service principal credentials"
- InitiatedBy.user.userPrincipalName = "victim_user@contoso.com"
- TargetResources[0].displayName = "HR-Automation-App"
- TargetResources[0].modifiedProperties includes PasswordCredentials change

## Phase 4: Privilege Escalation — Service Principal Authentication (T1078.004)
The attacker authenticates as the HR-Automation-App service principal:
POST /{tenant_id}/oauth2/v2.0/token
  grant_type=client_credentials
  client_id={hr-app-client-id}
  client_secret={injected-secret}
  scope=https://graph.microsoft.com/.default

This returns an application-level token with Mail.ReadWrite.All — escalating from the
victim user's limited delegated permissions to full tenant-wide mail access.

**Log evidence (AADSignInLogs):**
- ServicePrincipalId = "b2c3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f23456789012"
- ServicePrincipalName = "HR-Automation-App"
- IsInteractive = false
- ResultType = "0"
- IPAddress = attacker's IP (same as Phase 1)
- ResourceDisplayName = "Microsoft Graph"
- Category = "ServicePrincipalSignInLogs"

## Phase 5: Defense Evasion — Inbox Hiding Rule (T1564.008)
Before exfiltrating mail, the attacker creates an inbox rule on the CFO's mailbox:
POST /v1.0/users/{cfo_id}/mailFolders/inbox/messageRules

Rule configuration:
- Conditions: subject or body contains "security", "alert", "unusual sign-in", "password reset"
- Actions: Move to "RSS Feeds" folder, mark as read
- Purpose: Suppress security notifications that might alert the CFO to unauthorized access

**Log evidence (OfficeActivity):**
- Operation = "New-InboxRule"
- UserId = "cfo@contoso.com"
- ClientInfoString = "Client=Microsoft Graph;AppId=d4e5f6a7-b8c9-0123-defg-456789012345"
- Parameters: [{"Name": "MoveToFolder", "Value": "RSS Feeds"}, {"Name": "SubjectContainsWords", "Value": "security alert;unusual sign-in;password"}, {"Name": "MarkAsRead", "Value": "True"}]
- ResultStatus = "Succeeded"
- LogonType = 1 (Admin/Application access)

## Phase 6: Collection — Email Exfiltration (T1114.002)
The attacker reads the CFO's mailbox using application-level Graph token:
GET /v1.0/users/{cfo_id}/messages?$filter=receivedDateTime ge 2026-01-01&$top=999
GET /v1.0/users/{cfo_id}/messages/{id}/attachments

Targeted subjects: "acquisition", "board", "confidential", "wire transfer", "merger".
The attacker downloads 200+ emails with attachments over a 30-minute window.

**Log evidence (OfficeActivity):**
- Operation = "MailItemsAccessed"
- UserId = "cfo@contoso.com"
- MailAccessType = "Bind"
- ClientInfoString = "Client=Microsoft Graph;AppId=d4e5f6a7-b8c9-0123-defg-456789012345"
- ItemCount > 50 per event
- AppAccessContext.UniqueTokenId correlates with Phase 4 SP sign-in
- RecordType = 50
- SessionId consistent across all access events

